+# Agent Note: Provider families and configured instances
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-21-provider-families-and-instances.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The configurable-provider directory treated an installed adapter option, one stored configuration, and one model route as the same identity. Configuration clients therefore rendered dormant providers as rows, could not create two accounts from one implementation, and inferred configured state from settings paths. A display-name edit or a second endpoint could not receive an independent durable identity without inventing client-owned state.
+
+Credentials, settings, adapters, and active routes also had separate mutation APIs. A client could write settings and a credential but fail before validation or route publication, leaving a partial provider configuration whose recovery semantics belonged nowhere.
+
+## Decision
+
+`dsh-llm` distinguishes installed `LlmProviderFamily` records, configured `LlmProviderInstance` records, and active `LlmProviderInfo` routes. Family and instance IDs are branded opaque strings. An instance also carries a stable provider route used by requests and durable Session references; its editable display name does not participate in either identity.
+
+A provider plugin registers one family and a `LlmProviderFamilyController`. The family declares its settings namespace, the path of its instance dictionary, and a positive instance limit or `null` for no limit. The controller reads authoritative instances and owns save-and-test and deletion transactions. DSH serializes mutations per family, mints create identities, checks existence, and enforces the family limit before invoking the controller. Families with a single implementation use the same instance dictionary and set the limit to one, so later multi-instance support changes capability rather than persisted structure.
+
+The Remote API exposes family listing, instance listing, save-and-test, and deletion. A draft contains JSON-compatible non-secret configuration and an optional write-only credential action. Responses expose only `configured`, `missing`, or `not-required`; credential material is never returned. Provider implementations validate their own configuration, test connectivity, and publish settings, credentials, and routes as one owned operation or restore their preceding state before rejecting.
+
+`listProviders()` remains the active route projection. Configuration clients list families for Add Provider and instances for existing rows instead of joining active routes with the legacy configurable-provider directory. The payload-free topology event invalidates all three projections after registrations and committed instance mutations.
+
+## Identity and lifecycle
+
+Family registration is effect-scoped. Disposal withdraws the family before waiting for its in-flight mutation queue, preventing new operations while the controller reaches quiescence. Instance projections must preserve the registered family and settings namespace, provide non-empty IDs, route and display values, and use unique instance and route identities. Invalid controller output fails rather than entering a client projection.
+
+Deletion never grants the removed identity to a later create. Provider implementations retain tombstone or identity-generation information when their route format could otherwise repeat. Historical sessions keep their recorded route; an unavailable route remains unavailable rather than resolving to a newly created account.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Extend `LlmConfigurableProvider` with an optional instance key.** Rejected because the type would still combine an installed family, a configured record, and a route. Every consumer would need mode-dependent interpretation, and dormant rows would remain indistinguishable from instances.
+
+**Mount one Cordis plugin entry per configured account.** Rejected because user state would rewrite application composition, duplicate package lifecycle, and make a settings edit responsible for Loader transactions. One loaded provider package can own zero to many runtime instances directly.
+
+**Let Web and product shells coordinate settings and credentials.** Rejected because clients can disconnect between writes and cannot enforce family limits against concurrent clients. The provider implementation owns rollback because it alone can validate its fields, test its endpoint, and publish its adapter route.
+
+**Use the display name or settings-map key as the route.** Rejected because renaming would break Session references and deleting then recreating the same label could silently reinterpret history.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A clean installation can expose addable families while reporting no configured instances and no active model routes. Single-instance and multi-instance families share one storage and UI model. The service gains an operation controller in addition to the adapter registry, and provider implementations must implement transaction and identity-retirement behavior rather than relying on generic settings forms alone.
+
+The legacy configurable-provider directory remains temporarily for adapters and clients not yet migrated. New configuration clients use the family and instance projections; the directory can be removed after all shipped providers and consumers move.
+
+## Verification
+
+The LLM topology suite covers detached family metadata, separation from active routes, server-side limit enforcement under concurrent creates, stable route identity across rename, deletion by opaque instance ID, malformed controller output, and effect disposal. Provider packages add real Loader composition coverage for settings, credential, route, and rollback behavior. Client tests cover empty state, add limits, write-only credentials, save failures, restore, and topology invalidation.
