+Provider configuration separates three projections. `listProviderFamilies()` returns installed provider kinds available to an Add Provider surface, `listProviderInstances()` returns durable user-configured instances, and `listProviders()` returns only routes with a registered adapter. `LlmProviderFamily` carries an opaque branded id, display name, settings namespace and instance-dictionary path, plus a positive `maxInstances` or `null` for no limit. `LlmProviderInstance` carries a separate opaque id, its stable request route, editable display name, enabled and active state, settings address, write-only credential availability, and an optional diagnostic.
+
+A provider plugin registers one family with a `LlmProviderFamilyController`. DSH serializes creates, updates, and deletes per family, mints create identities, checks update/delete existence, and enforces the family limit before calling the controller. The controller owns settings, credential, endpoint-test, and route rollback as one operation. `saveProviderInstance()` accepts JSON-compatible provider configuration plus an optional `keep`, `replace`, or `remove` credential action and never returns credential material. The [provider-family decision](../../.agents/notes/implemented/architecture/2026-09-21-provider-families-and-instances.md) defines identity retirement and client ownership.
+
+The predecessor configurable-provider directory remains during migration. Adapter plugins using that API declare which routes *could* run through `registerConfigurableProviders()`, addressing each one's user-settings section, so older configuration surfaces can offer dormant providers before any route registers.
 
